id: pm-71v4l9
title: "Changelog safety for closed-item writes is gated on the wrong field: the generator buckets by release tag, so the risky population is 337 items and not the 1,475 the closed_at rule protects"
description: ""
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[5]: changelog,governance,history,measurement,release
created_at: "2026-08-13T08:36:36.894Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-13T08:44:18.296Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 180
acceptance_criteria: "The changelog-safety precondition is stated over the field the generator buckets by, and the statement cites the generator invocation that establishes it; A command classifies any candidate item set as changelog-safe or changelog-risky before the write, so a wave does not have to reason about it from prose; The risky population is reported as a number the project can watch, and shrinks as release attribution is backfilled rather than as closed_at is backfilled; A negative control proves a genuinely risky write is refused or flagged: touching a closed item with no release value must be distinguishable from touching one that has it; The operating documentation that currently states the closed_at rule is corrected, so the next wave does not re-derive the wrong premise"
goal: explainable-graph
objective: "The precondition for touching a closed item is stated over the field the changelog generator actually buckets by, and is checkable before the write"
value: "This one premise gates every graph enrichment and backfill wave that touches history. While it is stated over the wrong field it forbids 1,770 safe writes and says nothing useful about the 337 risky ones."
impact: "Unblocks pm-hnc9w7, pm-lnrk and the terminal-history enrichment waves, and removes pm-wrbe from the critical path of work it does not actually gate."
outcome: "A closed-item write is classified safe or risky by a command before it happens, denominated in the release field, and the classification is proven against a real changelog regeneration."
why_now: "Measured directly this session by regenerating the changelog after touching four closed items: the diff was fifteen lines of intra-section reordering and zero bucket movement."
parent: pm-33cw
risk: medium
confidence: 95
