  pm-lnrk,verifies,"2026-08-13T08:44:18.132Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
  pm-wrbe,verifies,"2026-08-13T08:44:18.132Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
body: "## The rule as operated\n\nThe standing rule is that touching a closed item updates `updated_at`, the changelog buckets by `updated_at`, and therefore any ad hoc closed-item repair silently drags years-old work into the current release notes. pm-hnc9w7's own body states it, and its `why_now` cites it as the reason the cleanup batch has never been scheduled. The refinement in the operating notes narrows it to closed items lacking `closed_at`.\n\n## What the generator actually does\n\nThe checked invocation, from `pnpm changelog:pm:check`:\n\n```\npm changelog generate --output CHANGELOG.md --title Changelog --mode replace \\\n  --all-release-tags --status closed --exclude-tag changelog-exclude \\\n  --item-url-base https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/main/.agents/pm\n```\n\n`--all-release-tags` buckets by the item's `release` field. `updated_at` orders rows *within* a bucket. So the field that decides which release section an item appears in is `release`, and `closed_at` is not read at all.\n\n## Measured, not reasoned\n\nFour closed items were touched during the graph hygiene batch: pm-7t04 and pm-v7dj (`release: v2026.5.18`), pm-1p2u (`v2026.6.22`), pm-zdec (`v2026.5.1`). All four lack `closed_at`, so the operating rule classifies every one of them as unsafe.\n\n`pnpm changelog:pm:check` failed as expected. Regenerating to a scratch file and diffing against the committed `CHANGELOG.md` produced fifteen lines:\n\n```\n1320a1321 > - SDK invoke helpers ... (pm-1p2u)\n1328d1328 < - SDK invoke helpers ... (pm-1p2u)\n2153a2154,2155 > - Add agent-optimized pm plan command ... (pm-v7dj)\n                > - Add pm init checks for AGENTS/CLAUDE ... (pm-7t04)\n2161d2162 < - Add agent-optimized pm plan command ... (pm-v7dj)\n2165d2165 < - Add pm init checks ... (pm-7t04)\n2586a2587 > - Add dependency mutation command ... (pm-zdec)\n2593d2593 < - Add dependency mutation command ... (pm-zdec)\n```\n\nEvery one of the four moved to the top of its own existing release section. No item entered `[Unreleased]`. No section gained or lost an item. The feared effect did not occur, and could not have: the bucket is a function of `release`, which the write did not touch.\n\n## The three populations\n\nCounted across the 2,107 closed item documents:\n\n- 1,770 carry a `release` value. Touching these can only reorder rows inside a section that is already published. Changelog-safe.\n- 337 carry no `release` value. These are the only writes whose bucket placement can move. Changelog-risky.\n- 1,475 lack `closed_at`. This is the population the operating rule protects.\n\n`closed_at` therefore over-counts the risky set by a factor of 4.4 and, worse, does not contain it: an item can have `closed_at` and no `release`, in which case the rule calls it safe and it is not.\n\nAll 98 `no-active-blocker` sentinel holders retired in this session's hygiene batch carry a `release` value. Every one was changelog-safe, and the operating rule had classified all 98 as unsafe because none of them has `closed_at`.\n\n## Consequence for the waves this gates\n\npm-hnc9w7 is estimated at 900 minutes and blocked partly on this premise. pm-lnrk's Wave 2 terminal-history backfill is scoped as \"changelog-safe, epoch-ordered batches\" for the same reason. pm-wrbe's deterministic `closed_at` backfill is genuinely valuable for point-in-time reads and history-derived ordering, but it is not the changelog gate it is recorded as being, and holding waves for it is holding them for the wrong reason.\n\nThe residual risk is real but small and computable: 337 items, addressable by backfilling release attribution rather than timestamps, and separately owned by the release-attribution work under pm-cpzt82."
